submit_final_report({'title': 'Ring of Nestor', 'sections': [{'title': 'Ring of 
Nestor', 'blocks': [{'text': 'The **Ring of Nestor** is a modern name applied to
one of the gold signet rings recovered from the so‑called Griffin Warrior tomb, 
a Late Bronze Age warrior’s grave discovered near the Mycenaean Palace of Nestor
at Ano Englianos, close to Pylos in southwestern Greece.[1][3][6] The grave, 
dated to the Mycenaean period Late Helladic IIA, contained four gold signet 
rings among an assemblage of more than 1,400 objects, including jewellery, 
weapons, armour, and precious metal vessels.[1][5][7] These rings, decorated 
with detailed imagery in a Minoan style, have been cited as important evidence 
for cultural interaction between Mycenaean and Minoan worlds, and one of them 
has come to be referred to in scholarship and popular accounts as the "Ring of 
Nestor" because of its association with the palace traditionally linked to the 
Homeric king Nestor.[2][5][8]', 'source_ids': ['20260627191649433192_fc8e6eca', 
'20260627191649493562_2472a682', '20260627191649532060_5f547e99'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The term "Ring of Nestor" does not appear in all 
descriptions of the grave goods, and some accounts refer more generally to one 
or more gold rings from the Griffin Warrior’s burial rather than to a single, 
uniquely named object.[1][3][9] As a result, the designation is used here as a 
convenient label for a gold signet ring from this context rather than as a 
formal museum title.', 'source_ids': ['20260627191649433192_fc8e6eca', 
'20260627191649493562_2472a682'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}], 
'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260627191650378223_777a0c0c'], 'section_type': 
''}, {'title': 'Discovery and archaeological context', 'blocks': [{'text': 'The 
Griffin Warrior tomb was discovered in 2015 in an olive grove approximately 200 
yards from the Mycenaean Palace of Nestor at Ano Englianos, near Pylos in 
southwestern Greece.[1][3][6] The burial was excavated by an international team 
of archaeologists led by researchers from the University of Cincinnati, who 
identified it as the grave of an elite warrior based on the weaponry and luxury 
objects interred with the body.[1][6]', 'source_ids': 
['20260627191649433192_fc8e6eca', '20260627191649493562_2472a682'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The grave contained more than 1,400 objects, including 
jewellery, weapons, armour, and vessels made of bronze, silver, and gold.[1] 
Among these grave goods were four gold rings and silver cups, along with bronze 
bowls, cups, jugs, and basins.[1][2] The presence of such rich material in a 
single burial near the Palace of Nestor has been taken as evidence for the high 
social status of the interred individual and for the significance of the site 
within the Mycenaean world.[1][5][6]', 'source_ids': 
['20260627191649433192_fc8e6eca', '20260627191649472002_2a604cd6', 
'20260627191649532060_5f547e99'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Scholarly 
analysis dates the Griffin Warrior’s tomb to the Mycenaean period known as Late 
Helladic IIA, linking it chronologically with early phases of the palace culture
at Pylos.[5][6] The combination of Mycenaean and Minoan‑style artifacts in the 
grave, including the gold signet rings, has made the burial a key reference 
point for discussions of cultural contacts across the Aegean during this 
period.[4][5][8]', 'source_ids': ['20260627191649493562_2472a682', 
'20260627191649511874_b28b7611', '20260627191649532060_5f547e99'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}], 'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260627191650378223_777a0c0c'], 
'section_type': ''}, {'title': 'Description', 'blocks': [{'text': 'Accounts of 
the Griffin Warrior’s grave goods consistently note the presence of four gold 
rings, usually described as signet rings.[1][2][5][7] At least one individual 
gold ring from the burial is explicitly catalogued in published inventories of 
artifacts from the tomb.[3] The rings are made of gold and are associated with 
the category of signet jewellery, implying a function related to sealing, 
display, or both.[4][5]', 'source_ids': ['20260627191649433192_fc8e6eca', 
'20260627191649472002_2a604cd6', '20260627191649493562_2472a682', 
