{"accepted": true, "revision_requested": false, "severity": "clean", "report": 
"# Ring of Nestor\n\n## Ring of Nestor\n\nThe **Ring of Nestor** is a modern 
name applied to one of the gold signet rings recovered from the so‑called 
Griffin Warrior tomb, a Late Bronze Age warrior’s grave discovered near the 
Mycenaean Palace of Nestor at Ano Englianos, close to Pylos in southwestern 
Greece. The grave, dated to the Mycenaean period Late Helladic IIA, contained 
four gold signet rings among an assemblage of more than 1,400 objects, including
jewellery, weapons, armour, and precious metal vessels. These rings, decorated 
with detailed imagery in a Minoan style, have been cited as important evidence 
for cultural interaction between Mycenaean and Minoan worlds, and one of them 
has come to be referred to in scholarship and popular accounts as the \"Ring of 
Nestor\" because of its association with the palace traditionally linked to the 
Homeric king Nestor. [1][3][5]\n\nThe term \"Ring of Nestor\" does not appear in
all descriptions of the grave goods, and some accounts refer more generally to 
one or more gold rings from the Griffin Warrior’s burial rather than to a 
single, uniquely named object. As a result, the designation is used here as a 
convenient label for a gold signet ring from this context rather than as a 
formal museum title. [1][3]\n\n## Discovery and archaeological context\n\nThe 
Griffin Warrior tomb was discovered in 2015 in an olive grove approximately 200 
yards from the Mycenaean Palace of Nestor at Ano Englianos, near Pylos in 
southwestern Greece. The burial was excavated by an international team of 
archaeologists led by researchers from the University of Cincinnati, who 
identified it as the grave of an elite warrior based on the weaponry and luxury 
objects interred with the body. [1][3]\n\nThe grave contained more than 1,400 
objects, including jewellery, weapons, armour, and vessels made of bronze, 
silver, and gold. Among these grave goods were four gold rings and silver cups, 
along with bronze bowls, cups, jugs, and basins. The presence of such rich 
material in a single burial near the Palace of Nestor has been taken as evidence
for the high social status of the interred individual and for the significance 
of the site within the Mycenaean world. [1][2][5]\n\nScholarly analysis dates 
the Griffin Warrior’s tomb to the Mycenaean period known as Late Helladic IIA, 
linking it chronologically with early phases of the palace culture at Pylos. The
combination of Mycenaean and Minoan‑style artifacts in the grave, including the 
gold signet rings, has made the burial a key reference point for discussions of 
cultural contacts across the Aegean during this period. [3][4][5]\n\n## 
Description\n\nAccounts of the Griffin Warrior’s grave goods consistently note 
the presence of four gold rings, usually described as signet rings. At least one
individual gold ring from the burial is explicitly catalogued in published 
inventories of artifacts from the tomb. The rings are made of gold and are 
associated with the category of signet jewellery, implying a function related to
sealing, display, or both. [1][2][3][5]\n\nBecause the available publications 
focus on the assemblage as a whole, detailed information on the precise 
dimensions and weight of the individual rings, including the one referred to 
here as the Ring of Nestor, is not consistently reported. Some descriptions 
mention a gold ring from the burial in general terms, while others emphasise the
group of four gold signet rings without distinguishing their individual sizes or
specific catalogue numbers. [1][3][5]\n\n## Iconography and artistic 
characteristics\n\nThe four gold signet rings from the Griffin Warrior’s tomb 
bear highly detailed imagery described as Minoan iconography. This description 
reflects the observation that the motifs, style, and compositional arrangements 
on the rings are closely comparable to those found on Minoan seals and signet 
rings from Crete and related sites. [4][5]\n\nScholars have highlighted the 
craftsmanship of these rings and their complex scenes as among the most 
informative examples of Aegean Bronze Age glyptic art, though individual 
publications differ in the level of detail they provide about specific motifs on
each ring. In discussions of the Griffin Warrior assemblage, the rings are 
frequently treated as a group, so the iconographic features of any one ring, 
including the Ring of Nestor, are typically summarised rather than described 
exhaustively. [3][4][5]\n\n## Scholarly interpretations and significance\n\nThe 
discovery of four gold signet rings with Minoan iconography in a Mycenaean 
warrior’s grave has been interpreted as strong evidence for sustained cultural 
interaction between Mycenaean communities on the Greek mainland and Minoan 
centres in Crete during the Late Bronze Age. The rings are discussed in relation
to broader questions about the transmission of artistic styles, religious 
symbols, and social practices across the Aegean. [4][5]\n\nIn one line of 
interpretation, the presence of Minoan‑style glyptic art in the Griffin 
Warrior’s tomb suggests that Mycenaean elites actively adopted and adapted 
Minoan iconography as part of their own expressions of status and identity. The 
ring referred to here as the Ring of Nestor is therefore situated within 
discussions of how mainland rulers engaged with, collected, or commissioned 
objects that drew on Cretan artistic traditions. [4][5]\n\nAnother interpretive 
emphasis links the rings to the symbolic and possibly ritual roles of signet 
jewellery in Late Bronze Age societies. Scholars have argued that signet rings 
such as those from the Griffin Warrior’s grave may have functioned not only as 
personal adornment or tools for sealing but also as markers of participation in 
particular social or religious networks. Within this framework, the Ring of 
Nestor is viewed as part of a wider corpus of gold signet rings that illuminate 
the intersection of power, religion, and artistic production in Mycenaean and 
Minoan contexts. [5]\n\nThe association of the Griffin Warrior’s burial with the
Palace of Nestor has also encouraged connections between the ring and later 
Homeric traditions about King Nestor of Pylos. While the archaeological evidence
does not identify the historical individual buried in the tomb with the literary
figure Nestor, the proximity of the grave to the palace complex traditionally 
linked to the Homeric king has influenced the modern naming of the artifact and 
the public interest it has attracted. [2][3]\n\nPublished discussions of the 
Griffin Warrior’s rings vary in the extent to which they single out individual 
pieces, and several accounts treat the four gold signet rings collectively 
rather than isolating a specific ring under a unique name. Available sources 
provide only limited detail on aspects such as precise dimensions, full 
ownership history after excavation, and comprehensive conservation or exhibition
records for the rings, including the object referred to here as the Ring of 
Nestor. As further scholarly work and museum documentation appear, more detailed
information about the artifact may become available. [1][3][5]\n\n## Provenance 
and display\n\nSources describing the Griffin Warrior’s grave goods emphasise 
their discovery in situ in the undisturbed burial near the Palace of Nestor, but
provide only limited information on the subsequent movement and curation of 
specific artifacts such as the Ring of Nestor. Publications focus primarily on 
the excavation context and analytical interpretation rather than on a detailed 
post‑excavation provenance trail for each object. [1][3][5]\n\nSimilarly, the 
available sources consulted for this article do not supply a comprehensive 
record of conservation treatments or an exhaustive list of exhibitions in which 
the Griffin Warrior’s rings have been displayed. Where public communication has 
occurred, it tends to present the rings as part of the wider assemblage of 
artifacts from the Griffin Warrior’s tomb, often highlighting their role in 
demonstrating the wealth and cultural connections of the buried individual 
rather than providing a separate exhibition history for each ring. 
[2][3][4][5]\n\n## References\n\n[1] Rare archaeological finds in Pylos 
https://www.archaeology.wiki/blog/2015/10/27/rare-archaeological-finds-pylos/\n\
n[2] Griffin Warrior Tomb 
https://griffinwarrior.org/griffin-warrior-tomb/\n\n[3] September/October 2019 
24 Artifacts from the Griffin Warrior's grave 
https://www.jstor.org/stable/pdf/26822963.pdf\n\n[4] Archaeologists unveil new 
findings from Greek warrior's tomb 
https://popular-archaeology.com/article/archaeologists-unveil-new-findings-from-
greek-warrior-s-tomb/\n\n[5] The Lord of the Gold Rings: The Griffin Warrior of 
Pylos 
https://www.academia.edu/121523110/The_Lord_of_the_Gold_Rings_The_Griffin_Warrio
